Enteropathic Arthritis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Asymmetric oligoarthritis, IBD symptoms, Inflammatory back pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-012
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of unilateral knee swelling and intense lower back pain in a patient with a known history of bloody diarrhea, resulting in an inability to weight-bear or sleep.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage revealed acute, severe large joint mono/oligoarthritis causing functional immobilization, occurring concurrently with a severe flare of presumed inflammatory bowel disease. An urgent after-hours assessment was required to rule out a concurrent septic joint, provide immediate localized and systemic pain relief, and evaluate the severity of the GI flare, as delaying until standard hours risked profound dehydration, joint destruction, and unmanageable pain.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My right knee is massively swollen, my lower back is throbbing, and my stomach cramps are terrible with all this bloody diarrhea."
History of Present Illness: The patient describes a 48-hour history of acute, rapidly worsening asymmetric oligoarthritis, primarily affecting the right knee and left ankle. The right knee is exquisitely painful with weight-bearing. Concurrently, the patient reports a severe exacerbation of chronic, bloody diarrhea (up to 8 times daily) and severe abdominal cramping. Furthermore, the patient complains of a deep, inflammatory back pain characteristic of sacroiliitis, which is worse at night and improves slightly with movement. Notes that the exacerbation of joint symptoms is directly paralleling the gastrointestinal flare. Reports uniform swelling of the right index finger (dactylitis) and pain at the back of the left heel (enthesitis).
Functional Status: Housebound, mobilizing only to the bathroom with extreme difficulty and pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of dehydration due to severe diarrhea; fall risk due to biomechanically compromised knee and ankle.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 18 bpm | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient looks pale, fatigued, and is visibly distressed, holding the right knee and guarding the abdomen.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: Right knee has a large, tense effusion, warm to touch, with globally restricted range of motion secondary to pain. Left ankle shows mild synovial thickening and tenderness. Right index finger displays classic "sausage digit" appearance (dactylitis). Exquisite point tenderness at the left Achilles tendon insertion (enthesitis). Bilateral sacroiliac joint tenderness elicited on palpation. Abdomen: Diffusely tender, particularly in the lower quadrants, hyperactive bowel sounds, no rebound or guarding. Dermatological: Tender, erythematous, nodular lesions on the anterior shins consistent with erythema nodosum. Diagnostic tetrad heavily represented clinically: profound asymmetric oligoarthritis, severe IBD symptoms (bloody diarrhea), and inflammatory back pain, anticipating future evidence of active Crohn's disease or ulcerative colitis on colonoscopy.
Environmental Observations: Clear path to bathroom required; patient advised on safe transfers.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking paracetamol with no joint relief; no current active IBD medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Enteropathic Arthritis - Status: Exacerbated (Concurrent joint and GI flare)
Differentials:

Reactive Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronic, ongoing nature of the bloody diarrhea (suggestive of IBD rather than a resolved acute infectious trigger) and the presence of erythema nodosum.

Septic Arthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The distinctly polyarticular/oligoarticular involvement (knee, ankle, dactylitis, back), the presence of clear systemic autoimmune skin signs (erythema nodosum), and the lack of a high swinging fever, though aspiration may eventually be needed if the knee fails to improve.

Ankylosing Spondylitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dominating presence of severe, active inflammatory bowel disease symptoms preceding and paralleling the peripheral arthritis.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is experiencing a severe, dual-system inflammatory flare characterized by immobilizing oligoarthritis and dehydrating, bloody diarrhea. The acute inability to mobilize safely combined with the systemic stress of an IBD flare necessitated an urgent after-hours physical assessment. It was critical to clinically distinguish the acute knee effusion from a superimposed septic joint and to instigate immediate, safe anti-inflammatory and gut-sparing management. Leaving this patient unassessed overnight carried unacceptable risks of severe dehydration, falls, and unmanageable distress, clearly meeting the requirements for the MBS 585-600 service.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Given the active, bloody gastrointestinal flare, NSAIDs are relatively contraindicated as they may severely exacerbate the IBD. Initiate an acute course of oral corticosteroids (e.g., Prednisolone 40mg daily) to simultaneously target the systemic joint inflammation and the gut flare. Recommend urgent stool cultures and fecal calprotectin via the regular GP, and arrange a rapid referral to both Gastroenterology and Rheumatology.
Positive Behavioral Change: Hydration pacing: Drink 100ml of oral rehydration solution every hour while awake; successfully achieving this specific, paced intake will demonstrably prevent the dizziness and tachycardia associated with dehydration from the diarrhea. Joint resting: Keep the right knee elevated and supported on a pillow; strictly avoiding weight-bearing will immediately reduce mechanical pain and prevent further reactive effusion.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None immediately.
DME/Safety (Optional): Walking aid (crutches) to offload the knee for necessary bathroom trips.
Follow-up: GP review within 24-48 hours. Gastroenterology review urgently required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if the abdominal pain becomes severe, rigid, or unremitting (risk of toxic megacolon or perforation), if you become confused/dizzy upon standing, or if the knee becomes intensely red and fevers spike above 38.5 C.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The severe inflammation in your joints is directly linked to the inflammation in your bowel. While steroids will start treating both, specialized tests from a gastroenterologist are needed to formally diagnose the bowel condition.
Clinical Course: Systemic steroids typically induce rapid improvement in both joint swelling and diarrhea. Long-term management will require specialized medications that control the immune system in both the gut and the joints.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

You must avoid anti-inflammatory tablets like Nurofen or Voltaren as they will make your bleeding bowel worse. Go to the hospital immediately if your stomach becomes hard and agonizingly painful, or if you feel faint when standing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Because your bowel is currently inflamed and bleeding, standard arthritis painkillers are unsafe. We are using high-dose steroids to aggressively switch off the immune attack in both your gut and your knee simultaneously.
Actionable Steps: Take the steroids strictly in the morning. Drink rehydration fluids constantly. Do not walk on the swollen knee without support.

